# 3 readings by claude-opus-5-5, compared
sidgwick-methods-4.md:1: argue/readings: S4 is read 2 ways: sidgwick-methods-4.logic.r1 and sidgwick-methods-4.logic.r1.2 have "aversion_egoistic"; sidgwick-methods-4.logic.r1.3 has nothing from it; they differ when nothing is true, which the second allows
sidgwick-methods-4.md:1: argue/answered: S4: answered no, which keeps sidgwick-methods-4.logic.r1 and sidgwick-methods-4.logic.r1.2, to: Suppose "Common Sense feels an aversion to the end of Egoistic rather than of Universalistic Hedonism" is false. Is S4, "It is, in fact, rather the end of Egoistic than of Universalistic Hedonism, to which Common Sense feels an aversion.", true then?
sidgwick-methods-4.logic.r1: argue/readings: what the text leaves unstated is read 2 ways: sidgwick-methods-4.logic.r1 and sidgwick-methods-4.logic.r1.2 have "lacks_ultimate → individual_unsatisfactory"; sidgwick-methods-4.logic.r1.3 has "offends → aversion_egoistic" and "lacks_ultimate → individual_unsatisfactory"; they differ when only "this offends both our sympathetic and our rational regard for others' happiness", "one's individual happiness does not possess the characteristics which we "divine" to belong to Ultimate Good" and "one's individual happiness is, in many respects, an unsatisfactory mark for one's supreme aim" are true, which the first allows
sidgwick-methods-4.md:1: argue/readings: the conclusion is read 2 ways: sidgwick-methods-4.logic.r1 has "universal_satisfies ∧ individual_unsatisfactory"; sidgwick-methods-4.logic.r1.2 and sidgwick-methods-4.logic.r1.3 have "individual_unsatisfactory"; they differ when only "one's individual happiness is, in many respects, an unsatisfactory mark for one's supreme aim" is true, which the second allows
sidgwick-methods-4.logic.r1: argue/readings: the main conclusion is read at 2 sentences: sidgwick-methods-4.logic.r1 puts it at S7, "But Universal Happiness, desirable consciousness or feeling for the innumerable multitude of sentient beings, present and to come, seems an End that satisfies our imagination by its vastness, and sustains our resolution by its comparative security."; sidgwick-methods-4.logic.r1.2 and sidgwick-methods-4.logic.r1.3 at S5, "And certainly one’s individual happiness is, in many respects, an unsatisfactory mark for one’s supreme aim, apart from any direct collision into which the exclusive pursuit of it may bring us with rational or sympathetic Benevolence."
sidgwick-methods-4.logic.r1: argue/answered: M: answered yes, which keeps sidgwick-methods-4.logic.r1, to: Is the text's main conclusion S7, "But Universal Happiness, desirable consciousness or feeling for the innumerable multitude of sentient beings, present and to come, seems an End that satisfies our imagination by its vastness, and sustains our resolution by its comparative security.", rather than S5, "And certainly one’s individual happiness is, in many respects, an unsatisfactory mark for one’s supreme aim, apart from any direct collision into which the exclusive pursuit of it may bring us with rational or sympathetic Benevolence."?
